Instead of a lazy segment tree, we can maintain the difference array $D[i] = f(i) - f(i-1)$ inside a Fenwick tree. 
Because $f(i)$ is strictly non-decreasing, $D[i] \ge 0$ is guaranteed at all times. This property perfectly enables us to use binary lifting directly on the Fenwick tree to find the first index where the prefix sum $f(i)$ is $\ge L_i$ and the first index where $f(i) \ge R_i + 1$ in $O(\log M)$ time per bound. 

Once the bounds `left` and `right` are determined, adding 1 to the interval in the difference array is just two point updates on the Fenwick tree: 
- $D[left] \mathrel{+}= 1$
- $D[right + 1] \mathrel{-}= 1$

This reduces the high overhead typical of Python lazy segment trees. Binary lifting on a Fenwick array unrolled into constants takes fractions of the time, scaling securely inside standard time constraints even for maximum bounds.

